const body = `<h1>1.8a — Casting: Slicing Doubles into Ints</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Discovery Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Your damage formula rolls <code>2.6</code> damage per hit. But your monster has
        <code>8</code> hit points, and hit points are whole numbers. What happens to the
        <code>.6</code>? Today you find out. <strong>Do not read anything first.</strong>
        Predict, then run.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment: Five Lines, Five Surprises</h3>
        <p>Create <code>CastingLab.java</code> with a main method. Type these five lines
        exactly. <strong>Before running</strong>, write your prediction for each line in
        your lab notes:</p>
        <pre><code>System.out.println(5 / 2);
System.out.println(5.0 / 2);
System.out.println(7 / 2.0);
System.out.println((int) 9.99);
System.out.println((int) (5.0 / 2));</code></pre>
        <p>Run it. Lines 1 and 4 surprise almost everyone. Compare with your predictions
        and write down the rule you think explains all five.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warn">
        <strong>The two rules you just discovered:</strong><br>
        1. <code>int / int</code> is <strong>integer division</strong>. It chops off the
        decimal. <code>5 / 2</code> is <code>2</code>, not <code>2.5</code>. No rounding,
        no warning. The .5 is just gone.<br>
        2. <code>(int)</code> in front of a value is a <strong>cast</strong>. It converts
        a <code>double</code> to an <code>int</code> by truncating (cutting, not
        rounding). <code>(int) 9.99</code> is <code>9</code>.
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment 2: The Casting Order Trap</h3>
        <p>Now predict these two lines. They look almost identical. They are not.</p>
        <pre><code>System.out.println((int) (Math.random() * 6) + 1);   // line A
System.out.println((int) (Math.random() * 6 + 1));   // line B</code></pre>
        <p>Run each 20 times. One of them can print <code>0</code>. Which one, and why?
        The parentheses decide whether the cast happens before or after the <code>+ 1</code>.
        Write the explanation in your own words. This exact trap appears on the AP exam
        almost every year.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment 3: Widening Goes Free, Narrowing Pays</h3>
        <pre><code>int hp = 8;
double d = hp;        // int -> double: automatic
System.out.println(d);

double ratio = 0.75;
// int copy = ratio;  // uncomment this line. What does the compiler say?
int copy = (int) ratio;   // double -> int: you must cast
System.out.println(copy);</code></pre>
        <p>Uncomment the middle line and read the compiler error. That error is the whole
        point: Java converts <code>int</code> to <code>double</code> silently (nothing is
        lost), but refuses <code>double</code> to <code>int</code> without a cast,
        because information WILL be lost. Java makes you say "I know" in writing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The Average That Lied</h3>
        <p>A student wrote this to average three combat scores. It compiles. It runs.
        It is wrong in a subtle way.</p>
        <pre><code>int s1 = 7, s2 = 8, s3 = 10;
int average = (s1 + s2 + s3) / 3;
System.out.println("Average score: " + average);</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>What does it print? Run it.</li>
            <li>What SHOULD it print?</li>
            <li>Fix it so the answer is <code>8.333333333333334</code>. You need exactly
            one cast. Where does it go? Try placing it in different spots and see how the
            answer changes.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Your RPG stores gold as <code>double</code> (loot can be fractional) but
            displays it as a whole number. Should you cast, or round? What does
            <code>(int) 2.9</code> give, and is that fair to the player?</li>
            <li><code>Math.random()</code> returns a <code>double</code> from 0.0 up to
            1.0. Explain, in one sentence each, what <code>(int)(Math.random() * 10)</code>
            and <code>(int)(Math.random() * 10) + 1</code> can produce.</li>
            <li>Look at the <a href="../docs/unit-01/casting.html">Casting docs page</a>.
            Find the "predict and confirm" section. Do the four mystery lines. How many
            did you get right?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Predict the result of <code>int / int</code>, mixed division, and
            <code>(int)</code> casts without running the code</li>
            <li>Explain why <code>(int) 9.99</code> is 9 and not 10</li>
            <li>Place a cast so a formula truncates at the right moment</li>
            <li>Read the compiler error for an implicit narrowing conversion</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/casting.html">Casting &amp; Integer Division</a> — the full story, plus more mystery lines</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/arithmetic.html">Arithmetic</a> — the operator rules behind all of this</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> — your exam sheet; find Math.random()</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
